  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses solely on comparing the maximum probability of each lottery and prefers the one with the higher value. In this case, Lottery A has a maximum probability of 100%, and Lottery B has 66%, so the reason would prefer A. The individual in the report does mention the certainty of Lottery A (100%) and contrasts it with the probabilistic nature of B, which aligns with attention to high probabilities. However, their justification is not based on maximizing the highest single probability per se, but rather on a risk-reward trade-off: the small increase in potential gain (100 Euros) is not worth even a 1% risk of nothing. This reflects a risk-averse cost-benefit evaluation, not a rule based purely on identifying and acting on the maximum probability. While the individual does recognize the certainty in A, their reasoning extends beyond the scope of the decision reason by incorporating outcome differences and subjective valuation of risk. Therefore, although there is partial overlap in considering high probability, the core justification does not match the rule of the decision reason.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
}

